===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{short for|en|acquis communautaire}}
## {{lb|en|law}} The [[accumulated#Adjective|accumulated]] [[legislation]], [[legal]] [[act#Noun|acts]], and [[court#Noun|court]] [[decision]]s which constitute the [[total#Adjective|total]] [[body]] of [[European Union]] [[law]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Christopher Preston|chapter=Conclusion: Can the Classical Method Survive?|title=Enlargement and Integration in the European Union|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Routledge}}|year=1997|page=228|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LklfielKQbYC&pg=PA228|isbn=978-0-415-12002-9|passage=Despite the clarity with which this principle has been reiterated, most applicants believe that their own circumstances are unique and deserve an equality of respect, even when this might challenge a principle of at least part of the '''acquis'''. The UK, for example, believed that its wider trading links merited special consideration. The realisation that such consideration would not be forthcoming was particularly humiliating, given that the UK had been invited to participate in forming the '''acquis''' in 1955–57.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Oxana Gutu|chapter=Conclusions and Recommendations|title=Moldova’s Convergence with the ''Acquis'': A Pro-growth and Pro-integration Strategy|series=CEPS Working Document|seriesvolume=no. 238|location=Brussels|publisher={{w|Centre for European Policy Studies}}|month=March|year=2006|page=28|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=tUXoRjwPCTkC&pg=PA28|isbn=978-92-9079-614-5|passage=[T]he process of absorption of the EU Internal Market '''''acquis''''' by the new EU member states (Hungary and Estonia, e.g. completed their alignment wih the Internal Market '''''acquis''''' within three years) is not feasible for the Moldovan economy, at least in the following three to five years.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Helmut Heiss|author2=Ulrike Mönnich|chapter=Pre-contractual Duties in European Insurance Contract Law|editors=Yong Qiang Han and Greg Pynt|title=Carter v Boehm and Pre-contractual Duties in Insurance Law: A Global Perspective after 250 Years|location=Oxford|publisher=[[w:Bloomsbury Publishing|Hart Publishing]]|year=2018|section=part IV (Pre-contractual Duties in Insurance Law: Mixed Legal System, Reinsurance and Brokers)|page=386|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=wD5bDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA386|isbn=978-1-50991-604-7|passage=In general, the existing ''acquis communautaire'' was adhered to as closely as possible in the drafting of the PEICL [Principles of European Insurance Contract Law]. Exceptions were made where shortcomings could be identified. Therefore, a number of directives, for example the insurance '''''acquis''''', have been taken into account in the PEICL.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Geriant Howells|author2=Mateja Durovic|chapter=The Rise of EU Consumer Law between Common Law and Civil Law Traditions|editor=Francisco de Elizade|title=Uniform Rules of European Contract Law?: A Critical Assessment|location=Oxford|publisher=[[w:Bloomsbury Publishing|Hart Publishing]]|year=2018|section=part III (Uniform Rules to Bridge the Gap between the Common Law and the Civil Law)|page=135|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=f4xaDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA135|isbn=978-1-50991-628-3|passage=It is a remarkable story of how the EU has managed to create an entire '''acquis''' that ensures that the whole of Europe speaks about consumer contract using the same terminology and policy framework.}}
## {{lb|en|international law}} The accumulated legislation and decisions of any international [[community]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Gerhard Erasmus|author2=Trudi Hartzenberg|author3=Paul Kalenga|chapter=Patterns of African Regional Integration: The Tripartite and Continental Free Trade Areas|editors=Achim Gutowski [''et al.'']|title=Africa’s Progress in Regional and Global Economic Integration&nbsp;– Towards Transformative Regional Integration|series=African Development Perspectives Yearbook 2015/16|seriesvolume=18|location=Zürich|publisher={{w|LIT Verlag}}|year=2016|page=87|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KjOSDQAAQBAJ&pg=PA87|isbn=978-3-643-90523-9|passage='''Acquis''' is a French term meaning "that which has been agreed". In the context of the Tripartite Free Trade Agreement (TFTA) means that the negotiations should start from the point at which {{...}} the COMESA, EAC and SADC trade negotiations have reached. Tariff negotiations and the exchange of tariff concessions would be among Member/Partner States of the Tripartite FTA that have no preferential arrangements in place between them. This will both preserve the '''acquis''' and build on it.}}

